Steward @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-steward, @ARCH@) - EARLY TEST RELEASE
=========================================================================

Steward manages OpenVMS from a web browser. It opens on a dashboard of the
system (OS and hardware, uptime, CPU, memory, paging, disks, processes, queues,
cluster), and covers users and rights identifiers, roughly what AUTHORIZE does:

  - sign in with your OpenVMS username and password (SYS$ACM: expiry, DISUSER,
    login hours and break-in evasion all apply); an expired password can be
    changed at sign-in
  - switch your authorized privileges on and off for the session, as
    SET PROCESS/PRIVILEGE does
  - list, filter and read users (everything AUTHORIZE SHOW shows) and rights
    identifiers with their holders
  - create users (as AUTHORIZE ADD), copy them (as COPY) and delete them (as
    REMOVE: with their UIC identifier and holdings)
  - modify users (owner, flags, days and hours, privileges, quotas, ...), set
    passwords, grant and revoke identifiers, create, rename and delete identifiers

Every request runs as you: Steward takes on your OpenVMS identity (a persona) for
the request, so OpenVMS decides what you may see and change, exactly as at a
terminal. Steward has no permissions of its own. Source, design and security
records: https://github.com/issinoho/vms-steward

This is a test release. It has been exercised on one x86-64 system. Please try it
on a test system first, and report problems at the address above.

Requirements: OpenVMS x86-64 (V9.2-x), VSI TCP/IP Services, VSI SSL3 (OpenSSL 3.0),
and lighttpd for OpenVMS (vms-lighttpd, https://github.com/issinoho/vms-lighttpd),
which serves the pages and passes the API to Steward. Browsers must reach Steward
over HTTPS: either through a reverse proxy that ends TLS (e.g. nginx proxy
manager), or with TLS in lighttpd.

Installed files (STEWARD$ROOT, defined by STEWARD$STARTUP)
---------------------------------------------------------
  [BIN]STEWARD.EXE             the FastCGI server (run as a pool of processes)
  [COM]STEWARD$CONFIGURE.COM   one-time setup, as SYSTEM
  [COM]STEWARD$CONTROL.COM     START, STOP, RESTART, STATUS
  [COM]STEWARD_POOL.COM        the pool (used by STEWARD$CONTROL)
  [COM]LOGIN.COM               copied to the service account (see below)
  [WWW]                        the browser front end (static files)
  [CONF]                       reference copies: STEWARD.CONF (annotated lighttpd
                               rules), STEWARD_SETTINGS.TEMPLATE
  [DOC]README.VMS, LICENSE
  SYS$STARTUP:STEWARD$STARTUP.COM, STEWARD$SHUTDOWN.COM

Setting up
----------
Install and set up vms-lighttpd first. Then, as SYSTEM:

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONFIGURE

It asks for (or takes as parameters) the service account (default STEWARD) and
its UIC, a data directory (default SYS$SYSDEVICE:[STEWARD_DATA]), the port lighttpd
should serve Steward on (default 8443), lighttpd's account, and any reverse
proxies to trust. It:

  - creates the service account: batch access only, RESTRICTED, DISCTLY, a
    random password nobody knows. Privileges IMPERSONATE, TMPMBX and NETMBX by
    default, and SYSPRV authorized but NOT default: Steward switches SYSPRV on
    only for the instant it creates a user's persona (OpenVMS needs it to read
    that user's UAF record), and checks after every request that it is off.
    An existing account keeps its UIC and password; its privileges are set as
    above, and its default directory becomes the data directory.
  - creates the data directory: LOGIN.COM (a RESTRICTED account must have one),
    [.RUN] (session store, audit log, settings, pool logs; private to the
    account), [.CONF] (the lighttpd rules)
  - makes a gateway secret (from OpenSSL's random generator), shared by the
    pool and lighttpd. lighttpd sends it with every API request and Steward
    refuses requests without it, so no other process on the system can talk to
    the pool directly and pretend to be a different client.
  - writes [.CONF]STEWARD.CONF, a lighttpd server block for the port, and the
    site settings, SYS$MANAGER:STEWARD$CONFIG.COM

Then add the line it prints to lighttpd's LIGHTTPD.CONF:

    include "/<device>/STEWARD_DATA/conf/steward.conf"

and:

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONTROL START
    $ @VMSLIGHTTPD$ROOT:[COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONTROL RESTART

Browse to https://<host>:<port>/ (or your proxy's address) and sign in.

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONTROL STATUS

shows the pool's processes and their privileges (they should be DETACH,TMPMBX,NETMBX:
SYSPRV off) and the end of the audit log.

Behind a reverse proxy
----------------------
Point the proxy at lighttpd's Steward port, and give STEWARD$CONFIGURE the address
the proxy connects from (it goes in STEWARD_SETTINGS.COM as
STEWARD_TRUSTED_PROXIES). Steward then believes the proxy's X-Forwarded-For,
-Proto, -Host and -Prefix headers, so break-in evasion and the audit log name the
real client. Never list an address untrusted clients can connect from.

The audit log
-------------
<data>[.RUN]AUDIT.LOG records every sign-in, sign-out, privilege switch and change,
with the user, the client address and OpenVMS's answer. Passwords are never
recorded.

Notes for this test release
---------------------------
- x86-64 only for now; IA64 will follow.
- Dates are shown in the browser's time zone. OpenVMS stores UAF dates in local
  time and keeps only the current offset from UTC, so a date from the other side
  of a daylight-saving change shows an hour out, as in other OpenVMS tools.
- Steward shows what OpenVMS lets you see, no more and no less: any signed-in
  user can read the rights database, and can tell whether a username exists,
  as at a terminal.

Removing
--------
    $ PRODUCT REMOVE STEWARD

stops the pool and deassigns STEWARD$ROOT. The data directory, the account and the
site settings are kept; remove the include line from LIGHTTPD.CONF by hand.
